# K. PRIVILEGED EFFECT DESCRIPTORS, AUTHORIZATION, AND POSITIVE ADMISSION

## PM-EFFECT-001 — Privileged Effect Descriptor

Every privileged Host Action kind and privileged Domain Transaction kind has a versioned descriptor in the active contract registry.

Each descriptor declares at least:

- effect kind and descriptor version;
- target type and target-set resolution semantics;
- required collection-closure grade;
- operation-scoped SupportAssessment requirement;
- minimum Security-State continuity;
- target-type-specific authorization attributes;
- required permission family;
- AssurancePredicate;
- preflight requirement/receipt type;
- DependencyGraph/ControlPath requirements;
- Impact/Protection/Risk requirements;
- Provider/executor binding and failover semantics;
- mutation-conflict domain;
- fence/commit semantics;
- replay/reconciliation class;
- execution deadline/lease;
- cancellation/compensation semantics;
- expected-disconnect scope;
- PostconditionPredicate;
- mandatory audit class;
- evidence durability/reserve requirements;
- evidence-strength floor where required;
- control-plane priority class;
- result/evidence disclosure-projection class.

Missing/unknown/incomplete descriptor blocks production admission.

## PM-PERM-001 — Permission Families

Prime defines granular families for ordinary inventory, sensitive inventory, process control, workload control, service control, resource control, diagnostics, export/externalization, protected reads, and recovery/transition requests.

## PM-IDTAX-001 — Identifier / Precondition / Authority Taxonomy

Identifiers such as Host/workload/process/action/transaction IDs are not credentials. Revision/fence/preflight tokens are integrity/precondition material, not authority. Cursors are continuation state. Grants/credentials are authority material.

## PM-AUTHZ-001 — AuthorizationDecision

Core creates the final authoritative AuthorizationDecision at the actual admission or protected-disclosure commit boundary from current inputs. It is not a reusable standing “allow.”

## PM-AUTHZ-002 — AuthorizationDecision Inputs

Can include principal/session/client, permission, target-type-specific security attributes, target revisions, support/compatibility, impact, protection, risk, assurance, Security-State continuity, policy, and classification.

## PM-AUTHZ-003 — Decision Snapshot for Evidence

Durable Action/Audit records preserve an immutable decision snapshot/reference with relevant input revisions and stable reason so later policy change does not erase why the operation was allowed at the time.

## PM-CAB-001 — Control Authority Bundle

Normal production control binds one complete authorized generation of the Prime contract set, authorization/security semantics, support authority, evidence/audit law, wire/schema generation, Provider mutation contracts, and relevant protection/risk semantics.

## PM-CAB-002 — No Partial Activation

Normal production cannot combine half-upgraded safety generations. Candidate bundles are Test/Developer-eligible only.

## PM-CAB-003 — CAB Activation Authority

The first production CAB is rooted in pre-existing Prime release/security authority plus explicit owner/frozen-architecture approval. A CAB cannot self-activate.

## PM-CAB-004 — CAB Lifecycle

CAB states distinguish candidate, Test/Developer eligible, production-active, superseded, revoked/retired as applicable. Terminal states do not resurrect.

## PM-ADMIT-001 — Positive Control Admission Law

Prime may admit a privileged effect only when:

- exact Decision World is coherent;
- current CAB is valid;
- effect descriptor is known/current;
- target identity/fences are valid;
- required collections satisfy closure grade;
- mandatory UNKNOWN is absent;
- contradictions are resolved or explicitly block;
- every Admission Obligation closes;
- SupportAssessment satisfies descriptor requirement;
- assurance predicate is satisfied;
- protection permits admission;
- risk requirements are satisfied;
- mandatory audit/evidence capacity is available;
- executor/Provider fencing is valid;
- Security-State continuity satisfies the descriptor.

“No blocker found” is insufficient.

